    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWe can use Thales' theorem to calculate missing angles in inscribed triangles. For example, consider the figure below. We have a triangle inscribed in a circle, and [MATH: AC] is a diameter.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/2973b64b22c0536512fd06614a603caf]\n\nContent:\nBecause [MATH: \\triangle ABC] is an inscribed triangle and its side [MATH: AC] is a diameter of the circle, Thales' theorem tells us that [MATH: \\triangle ABC] must be a right triangle.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/ab715f13ca2348a840d22f544c2b3e24]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Finding Measures of Angles in Inscribed Right Triangles\n\nExample Prompt:\n[IMG: /graphics/2fe269e0903e6513fefb885d0c15c07b]\n\nFind the value of [MATH: x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nNotice that [MATH: \\triangle{ABC}] is inscribed in the circle, where the side [MATH: \\overline{AB}] is a diameter. Therefore, by Thales' theorem, we have\n[MATH: m\\angle{ACB}=90^\\circ.]\n\nNow, using the fact that [MATH: \\triangle{ABC}] is a right triangle, we obtain\n\n[MATH: m\u2220BAC + m\u2220ABC|= 90^{\u2218}; (2x) + (2x + 10^{\u2218})|= 90^{\u2218}; 4x + 10^{\u2218}|= 90^{\u2218}; 4x|= 80^{\u2218}; x|= 20^{\u2218}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-53923]\n\nPrompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: x.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 14^{\u2218}]\nb. [MATH: 10^{\u2218}]\nc. [MATH: 12^{\u2218}]\nd. [MATH: 24^{\u2218}]\ne. [MATH: 21^{\u2218}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-53944]\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is the measure of [MATH: \u2220VWX?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 35^{\u2218}]\nb. [MATH: 40^{\u2218}]\nc. [MATH: 20^{\u2218}]\nd. [MATH: 45^{\u2218}]\ne. [MATH: 30^{\u2218}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Finding the Measure of an Angle Inside a Circle\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the measure of [MATH: \\angle{EBC}.]\n\n[IMG: /graphics/ba43afe049b07facd6f3cc85409094c1]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we compute\n\n[MATH: m\u2220DAB|= m\u2220DAE + m\u2220EAB; = 21^{\u2218} + 45^{\u2218}; = 66^{\u2218}.]\n\nNotice that [MATH: \\triangle{ADB}] and [MATH: \\triangle{ACB}] are inscribed in the circle, where the side [MATH: \\overline{AB}] is a diameter. Therefore,\n\n[MATH: m\\angle{ADB}=m\\angle{ACB}=90^\\circ.]\n\n- For [MATH: \\triangle{ADB}], we obtain\n[MATH: m\u2220ABD|= 90^{\u2218} \u2212 m\u2220DAB; = 90^{\u2218} \u2212 66^{\u2218}; = 24^{\u2218}.]\n- For [MATH: \\triangle{ACB}], we have\n[MATH: m\u2220ABC|= 90^{\u2218} \u2212 m\u2220CAB; = 90^{\u2218} \u2212 45^{\u2218}; = 45^{\u2218}.]\n\nFinally,\n\n[MATH: m\u2220EBC|= m\u2220ABC \u2212 m\u2220ABD; = 45^{\u2218} \u2212 24^{\u2218}; = 21^{\u2218}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-53992]\n\nPrompt:\nFind the measure of [MATH: \u2220AEB.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 135^{\u2218}]\nb. [MATH: 120^{\u2218}]\nc. [MATH: 145^{\u2218}]\nd. [MATH: 130^{\u2218}]\ne. [MATH: 140^{\u2218}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-53817]\n\nPrompt:\nFind the measure of [MATH: \u2220EBP.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 40^{\u2218}]\nb. [MATH: 50^{\u2218}]\nc. [MATH: 35^{\u2218}]\nd. [MATH: 20^{\u2218}]\ne. [MATH: 25^{\u2218}]"
